course: dictionary
lesson: 14147
title: Mislaid: put somewhere and not found
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words are gentler than others. Mislaid means you put something down somewhere and now cannot find it, but you still hope to find it again. «I have mislaid my glasses, but they must be on the kitchen table somewhere.» The word tells you the glasses are not really gone, just hidden for now.
explain: Compare it with «lost». «My keys are lost» can sound more serious, as if they might be gone for good. «My keys are mislaid» sounds calmer, like a small mix-up. Mislaid is the past form of the verb mislay, and it can also describe a thing: «The mislaid homework turned up in my bag.»
explain: Use the clues in the sentence. Look for words like «put», «somewhere» or «could not find». Then swap in your meaning: «I have put my glasses somewhere and cannot find them.» If it still makes sense, you have got it right.
fact: Mislaid means put something somewhere and then could not find it.
fact: «Mislaid» is the past form of the verb «mislay»: I mislay, I mislaid, I have mislaid.
fact: «Mislaid» is a gentler word than «lost», because you hope to find the thing again.
fact: «Mislaid» can also describe something that is lost for a while because it was put in the wrong place.
fact: Some people say «I seem to have mislaid my hat», which sounds polite or old-fashioned.
exercise: «I have mislaid my school bag somewhere in the classroom.» What does mislaid mean here? | *Put somewhere and cannot be found for now | Broken into small pieces | Stolen by a thief and gone for ever | Carried very carefully
exercise: Which word is the past form of the verb mislay? | mislays | *mislaid | mislaying | mislay
exercise: Mum says «I have mislaid the car keys», not «I have lost the car keys». Why is mislaid the gentler choice? | Because mislaid means broken | Because the keys are definitely gone for ever | Because keys are not important | *Because you still hope to find the keys
test: Dad said, «I seem to have mislaid my hat», and smiled. Which word tells you the hat is probably not gone for good? | seem | said | *mislaid | smiled
test: In «The mislaid homework turned up in my bag», what does mislaid describe? | *the homework | the bag | the teacher | the morning
exam: Which meaning best fits mislaid? | *Put somewhere and could not find it for a while | Given away to a friend | Torn into pieces and thrown out | Read very quickly from start to finish
